Lobium — native mediaDevices enumerateDevices spoof (policy.mediaDevices).

Replaces the host device list with persona camera/microphone/speaker counts, shaped the way the
browser process shapes the real one: a single blank entry per kind until the frame holds the capture
permission for it, and hashed deviceIds with OS-shaped labels and groupIds once it does.

CAPABILITIES, not just identity. enumerateDevices always asks for input capabilities - Blink
hardcodes request_video_input_capabilities=true and enables the audio one by default off mac - and
every real Chrome answers getCapabilities() with width/height/aspectRatio/frameRate/facingMode on a
camera and channelCount/sampleRate/latency/echoCancellation on a microphone. The persona devices
were constructed with only (id, label, groupId, kind), so getCapabilities() returned an almost-empty
dictionary: a device that enumerates but claims to be capable of nothing, which no real device is.

The values describe the same modest integrated hardware the labels already claim - a 720p webcam
with no pan/tilt/zoom, and a mono 48 kHz microphone - rather than a maximal set. facingMode is
stated only on Android, where front/back means something; a desktop camera reports none, which is
what Chrome does.
